  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Businessman E. Clayton Gengras helped create a school for children with intellectual disabilities by offering Saint Joseph College a conditional donation. His pledge depended on the college establishing a centre on its campus. His cousin’s sustained work with disabled people had encouraged him to support the project.\n\nThe college accepted, and Gengras fulfilled his pledge. Classes began in temporary premises with twenty pupils after Connecticut approved a special-education curriculum. When the purpose-built Gengras Center opened in 1965, it had forty-six students and seven full-time staff. The school also became a practical setting for university students learning to work in special education.",
    "html": "<p>Businessman E. Clayton Gengras helped create a school for children with intellectual disabilities by offering Saint Joseph College a conditional donation. His pledge depended on the college establishing a centre on its campus. His cousin’s sustained work with disabled people had encouraged him to support the project.</p>\r\n<p>The college accepted, and Gengras fulfilled his pledge. Classes began in temporary premises with twenty pupils after Connecticut approved a special-education curriculum. When the purpose-built Gengras Center opened in 1965, it had forty-six students and seven full-time staff. The school also became a practical setting for university students learning to work in special education.</p>\r\n",
    "word_count": 101,
